speechtext:
I quite agree with what the hon. gentleman has said, but I want to know what is the use of going through all this farce of making a pretence of examining these bills. We are not gaining anything by it; we are not obtaining sufficient evidence. If the hon. member for Winnipeg North Centre wants to get sufficient evidence in this house would he like to have all these bills discussed in detail? Then all this evidence would be published and scattered all over the land from Halifax to Vancouver. If we are to adopt that course the evidence will be printed in Hansard and will find its way into every corner of the country. Would that make for morality and good living?
I have been with the hon. member for Winnipeg North Centre up to a certain point;
I admit that the whole thing is a farce and that it is ridiculous that this house should be called upon to make these decisions. But the hon. member has made his protest; he has made it clear that we have come to a place beyond which we cannot go and that before another session comes round something must be done to meet the situation. He has gained his point; I think the country and this house realize that he is right, so why continue this thing? As the hon. member for Saskatoon has suggested, why not let these bills go through for this year, because they will go through anyway; then next year perhaps we will get something to meet the situation in the right way. I cannot see the use of wasting all this time.
